      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which Gulf countries accept an apostille?",
            "answer": "<p>Saudi Arabia (since December 2022), Oman (since 2012) and Bahrain (since 2013) are part of the Apostille Convention. The UAE, Qatar and Kuwait are not, so documents need full legalisation: your home authority, their embassy, then their foreign ministry.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need a licence to teach in the UAE?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. UAE law requires teachers to hold a teaching licence from the Ministry of Education. It involves subject and professional exams, and you get three attempts before a six-month wait. If you hold a licence from another country, you can apply to have it recognised.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are Gulf teaching salaries tax-free?",
            "answer": "<p>None of the six countries taxes employment income today. Oman will tax total income above OMR 42,000 a year at 5% from 2028, which most teachers will be below. Bahrain deducts 1% social insurance from expat salaries.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do South African teachers need SAQA for the Gulf?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, for verification. DIRCO won't legalise a South African degree without a SAQA verification letter, whatever the destination. We found no Gulf country that requires a separate SAQA evaluation.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is end-of-service pay?",
            "answer": "<p>A payment set by each country's labour law when your contract ends. In Saudi Arabia it is half a month's wage for each of your first five years, then a month's wage for each year after, reduced if you resign before ten years. Check how your contract calculates it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I switch schools mid-year in Dubai?",
            "answer": "<p>If you leave a Dubai private school mid-term, you must wait 90 days before starting at another Dubai private school, even if you serve your notice. Plan any move for the end of a term or year.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I teach in a Kuwaiti government school?",
            "answer": "<p>Kuwait's Ministry of Education recruits non-Kuwaiti teachers with conditions. For 2025/26 these included being 45 or under, two to three years' experience and certified transcripts. Private and international schools set their own criteria.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which Gulf country is best for teachers?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on your priorities: family rules, savings, licensing and how easily you can change jobs. Take the free assessment, or compare up to five destinations side by side with the Country Comparison Tool.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
